<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Aden Protectorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1872 CE – 1967 CE (approx.) · capital Aden</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's patchwork of some two dozen protected sultanates, emirates and sheikhdoms in the hinterland of Aden, the core of later South Yemen.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>To secure the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aden-settlement">Aden Settlement</a> and keep the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> away from it, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> signed treaties with the tribal rulers around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> from the 1870s, beginning with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-lahej">Sultanate of Lahej</a> and its neighbours, and later with the Hadrami states. Each ruler gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> control of his foreign relations in return for protection and stipends.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> at first intervened little in internal affairs. From 1937 the territory was divided into a Western and an Eastern Aden Protectorate, with political officers and local forces such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> Levies. In <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Hadramawt</a> the agent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-ingrams">Harold Ingrams</a> negotiated a truce among hundreds of feuding tribes in 1937, known as Ingrams' Peace. An Anglo-Ottoman line of 1904-1914 marked the border with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1959 six western states formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-arab-emirates-of-the-south">Federation of Arab Emirates of the South</a>, later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-south-arabia">Federation of South Arabia</a>, which most of the others joined; the eastern states, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quaiti-sultanate">Qu'aiti Sultanate</a>, remained outside. All were swept away by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> in 1967 and absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aden-protectorate.jpg" alt="Aden Protectorate" width="320" height="315"><figcaption>Image: Lubiesque · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aden_Colony_dependencies.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1872 CE – 1967 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aden</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Harold Ingrams</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Western Aden Protectorate, Eastern Aden Protectorate, Protectorate of South Arabia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=aden-protectorate" aria-label="Aden Protectorate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5975 1597 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aden Protectorate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6295 1853L4997 651M6295 1853L6236 1957M6295 1853L6247 1946M6295 1853L6252 1934M6295 1853L6256 1915M6295 1853L6252 1934M6295 1853L6226 1924M6295 1853L6273 1938M6295 1853L6242 1941M6295 1853L6240 1919M6295 1853L6272 1894M6295 1853L6273 1901M6295 1853L6223 1933M6295 1853L6222 1967M6295 1853L6224 1932M6295 1853L6225 1924M6295 1853L6262 1915M6295 1853L6319 1914M6295 1853L6330 1840M6295 1853L6344 1893M6295 1853L6411 1846"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M6236 1957h0M6247 1946h0M6252